---
// The full technical write-up behind "See the formula". Maths is rendered at build time by KaTeX (see ./tex.ts).
import { rich, tex } from './tex';
import { getCourse } from '../../lib/data/server';
import { simulate, pInAt, recommendBid } from '../../lib/sim/index';
import { toSimParams, FORECAST_RUNS, FORECAST_SEED } from '../../lib/sim/forecast';
import { eS, pct } from '../course/fmt';
import type { ForecastWindow } from '../../lib/data/types';

type Block = string | { ul: string[] } | { math: string } | { table: { head: string[]; rows: string[][] } };
interface Sec { id: string; h: string; blocks: Block[] }
const R = String.raw;

const sections: Sec[] = [
  {
    id: 'vacancy',
    h: '1. Data and observations',
    blocks: [
      R`One observation is one row of BOSS Overall Results: a section in one bidding window of one term.`,
      { table: { head: ['Field', 'Definition'], rows: [
        [R`seats @[S]@`, R`seats offered in that window (the vacancy before bids were processed), not the section's total capacity`],
        [R`successful @[B]@`, R`@[S]@ minus the vacancy after processing`],
        ['filled', 'vacancy after processing is 0'],
        [R`<code>mn</code>, <code>med</code>`, 'minimum and median of the successful bids, in cents'],
        [R`floor @[F]@`, R`1000 cents, unless a course's lowest observed minimum is e$11 to e$20 and at least 3 rows sit within a cent of it`],
        ['slot', 'start time of the first meeting (see Slot key below), plus <code>_fri</code> if the first listed day is Friday'],
      ] } },
      R`Rows are dropped if @[S\le 0]@, the vacancy after processing is negative or above @[S]@, the term is not Term 1 or Term 2 (3A and 3B are tiny), or the window is not one of the 11 listed. Windows are matched on BOSS's exact label, so each window has its own parameters. Rows from the term being forecast, or later, are never used for training.`,
      R`<b>Censoring.</b> If the section did not fill, every bidder succeeded, so @[N=B]@ exactly. If it filled, only @[N\ge S]@ is known (right-censored). For the clearing bid: if the section did not fill, every bid at or above the floor succeeds, so the clearing bid is the floor; if it filled, it is the lowest successful bid <code>mn</code> (pay-as-bid, highest bids win, ties random). The backtest target is therefore @[a=\texttt{mn}]@ if filled, else @[F]@.`,
      R`<b>Seats for the open term</b> come from a live scan of BOSS class search. It does not show every section to every student, so some sections cannot be scanned live. For those, marked ≈, seats are estimated from the previous window's results (seats left after processing). The estimate can be wrong if reserved seats are released or students drop, and an estimated zero may not be full.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'demand',
    h: '2. Demand model',
    blocks: [
      R`The number of bidders is negative binomial: a mean plus extra spread, so a busy section can be much busier than average.`,
      { math: R`N\sim\mathrm{NegBin}(\text{mean }\lambda,\ \text{size }r),\qquad \Pr(N=c)=\frac{\Gamma(c+r)}{\Gamma(r)\,c!}\left(\frac{r}{r+\lambda}\right)^{r}\left(\frac{\lambda}{r+\lambda}\right)^{c},\qquad \mathrm{Var}\,N=\lambda+\lambda^2/r .` },
      R`The dispersion @[r]@ is one global constant chosen in calibration (currently @[r=0.4]@, a heavy-tailed count).`,
      R`<b>Likelihood of one observation</b> @[o]@ with weight @[w_o]@, where @[\lambda_o=\lambda\,e^{\beta^d(\text{slot}_o,w)}]@ (section 5):`,
      { math: R`\ell_o(\lambda)=w_o\cdot\begin{cases}\log\Pr(N=B_o\mid\lambda_o) & \text{unfilled}\\ \log\Pr(N\ge S_o\mid\lambda_o)=\log\Big(1-\sum_{j<S_o}\Pr(N=j\mid\lambda_o)\Big) & \text{filled}\end{cases}` },
      R`The survival term is floored at @[10^{-300}]@. The likelihood is computed on a grid @[x_j=\log\lambda_j=\ln 0.02+0.1\,j]@, @[j=0..107]@, shifted by @[\mathrm{round}(\beta^d/0.1)]@ grid steps for the observation's slot. The posterior over the grid is`,
      { math: R`p(x_j)\;\propto\;\exp\Big(\sum_o \ell_o(e^{x_j})-\frac{(x_j-\mu)^2}{2\tau^2}\Big),` },
      R`which returns @[E[x]]@, @[\mathrm{sd}(x)]@ and @[E[e^x]]@. @[\mu]@ and @[\tau]@ come from the hierarchy (section 4).`,
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'bids',
    h: '3. Bid model',
    blocks: [
      R`Each of the @[N]@ bidders is, independently, either anchored or not:`,
      { ul: [
        R`with probability @[q]@ (anchor share) the bid is @[F+200\,U]@ cents, @[U\sim\mathrm{Unif}(0,1)]@: the minimum bid or just above it;`,
        R`otherwise the bid is @[M\,e^{\sigma Z}]@, @[Z\sim N(0,1)]@, where @[M=m\,e^{s_M\,Z_M}]@ and @[Z_M\sim N(0,1)]@ is drawn once per replay, so the whole window's price level shifts together.`,
      ] },
      R`Bids are rounded to whole cents and raised to @[F]@ if below it. The clearing bid is the @[S]@-th highest bid (@[k]@-th for groups, section 6).`,
      R`<b>Order-statistic scores.</b> For a filled section, take the course-level posterior mean @[\bar\lambda_c=E[\lambda]]@ for that window, @[\hat N=\max\big(S,\ E[N\mid N\ge S]\big)]@ and @[n_s=(1-q)\hat N]@ non-anchored bidders. The expected normal score of the @[k]@-th highest is`,
      { math: R`z(k)=\Phi^{-1}\!\Big(\mathrm{clip}_{[0.02,\,0.98]}\frac{n_s-k+0.625}{n_s+0.25}\Big),\qquad z_S=z(S),\quad z_{\mathrm{med}}=z\big(\tfrac{S+1}{2}\big),` },
      R`so that @[\ln(\text{clearing bid})\approx\ln m+\sigma z_S]@.`,
      R`<b>Spread @[\sigma_w]@ (per window).</b> The median successful bid is the @[\frac{S+1}{2}]@-th highest and the minimum successful bid is the @[S]@-th, so @[\ln(\texttt{med}/\texttt{mn})\approx\sigma\,(z_{\mathrm{med}}-z_S)]@. Over filled sections with @[\texttt{mn}>F+200]@ and @[\texttt{med}>\texttt{mn}]@:`,
      { math: R`\hat\sigma_w=\mathrm{clip}_{[0.15,\,0.9]}\frac{\sum_o w_o\ln(\texttt{med}_o/\texttt{mn}_o)}{\sum_o w_o\,(z_{\mathrm{med},o}-z_{S,o})},` },
      R`or 0.4 if the denominator is at most 3.`,
      R`<b>Anchor share @[q_w]@ (per window).</b> Take unfilled sections with @[B\ge 3]@ and @[\texttt{med}>0]@, and let @[f]@ be the weighted share whose median is within @[F+200]@. Under the model, the median of @[B]@ bids sits in the anchor band when at least half of them are anchored, so @[g(q)=\overline{\Pr[\mathrm{Bin}(B,q)\ge \lfloor (B+1)/2\rfloor]}]@ (weighted mean over those sections). Then @[q_w=\arg\min_{q\in\{0,0.05,\dots,0.9\}}|g(q)-f|]@. With fewer than 30 such sections, @[q_w=0.3]@.`,
      R`<b>Residual sd @[s]@.</b> The pooled within-(course, instructor, window) standard deviation of @[y_o=\ln \texttt{mn}_o-\sigma_w z_{S,o}]@ over filled sections with @[\texttt{mn}>F+200]@, floored at 0.2 (0.4 with 10 or fewer degrees of freedom). Currently @[s=0.373]@, one number for all windows.`,
      R`<b>Price level.</b> @[x=\ln m]@ per (window, group) has a Gaussian prior from the hierarchy and a Tobit likelihood over filled sections only:`,
      { math: R`y_o=\ln\texttt{mn}_o-\sigma_w z_{S,o}-\beta^p(\text{slot}_o,w)\sim N(x,\ s^2)\quad\text{if }\texttt{mn}_o>F+200,` },
      R`and if @[\texttt{mn}_o\le F+200]@ the clearing bid is hidden in the anchor band, so the term is left-censored, @[w_o\log\Phi\big((y^c_o-x)/s\big)]@ with @[y^c_o=\ln(F+200)-\sigma_w z_{S,o}-\beta^p]@. The grid is @[\ln 500+0.05\,j]@, @[j=0..89]@.`,
      R`<b>Prediction</b> at the course × instructor group @[ci]@:`,
      { math: R`\lambda=E[e^{x}\mid ci]\;e^{\beta^d},\qquad m=\max\!\big(F,\ e^{E[\ln m\mid ci]+\beta^p}\,c_{\text{scale}}\big),\qquad \sigma=\hat\sigma_w,\quad q=q_w,\quad s_M=\sqrt{\ell_{sd}^2+\mathrm{sd}(\ln m\mid ci)^2},` },
      R`where @[c_{\text{scale}}=1.350]@ (price scale from calibration) and @[\ell_{sd}=0.1]@ (term-to-term drift of the price level). Demand uses the posterior mean on the natural scale; price uses the posterior mean on the log scale (a median).`,
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'history',
    h: '4. Professor and course history (partial pooling)',
    blocks: [
      R`<b>Hierarchy.</b> Four levels, each fitted inside one window: all courses @[g]@, subject @[s]@, course @[c]@, course × instructor @[ci]@. Every observation is added to the group at all four levels. Each group's posterior is a grid posterior (section 2) whose Gaussian prior is centred on its parent's posterior mean:`,
      { math: R`\mu_{\text{group}}=E[x\mid\text{parent}],\qquad \mu_g=\ln 5\ (\text{demand}),\ \ln 2000\ (\text{price}),\qquad \tau_{\text{level}}.` },
      { table: { head: ['Level', R`@[\tau_d]@ (log @[\lambda]@)`, R`@[\tau_p]@ (log @[m]@)`], rows: [
        [R`@[g]@`, '3.0', '3.0'], [R`@[s]@`, '1.0', '0.5'], [R`@[c]@`, '0.9', '0.4'], [R`@[ci]@`, '0.7', '0.3'],
      ] } },
      R`A group with no observations returns its prior, so a new instructor inherits the course, a new course inherits the subject, and so on. A small @[\tau]@ keeps the group close to its parent. With @[n]@ weighted observations the data pull is roughly @[n/(n+\kappa)]@ with @[\kappa=2]@.`,
      R`<b>Time decay and cross-term weight.</b> For a target term with index @[t^*]@ and an observation from term @[t_o]@ (index @[t=2\cdot\text{start year}+\text{term}-1]@):`,
      { math: R`w_o=\delta^{(t^*-t_o)/2}\cdot\begin{cases}1&\text{same term type}\\ c_w&\text{otherwise}\end{cases},\qquad\delta=0.7\text{ per year},\ c_w=0.5.` },
      R`The Term 1 to Term 2 weight @[c_w]@ applies both ways: Term 2 results count half as much when forecasting Term 1, and Term 1 results count half as much when forecasting Term 2. Weights multiply each observation's log-likelihood, as fractional counts. Terms with @[w\le10^{-3}]@ are dropped.`,
      R`<b>Pooling level.</b> The level shown for a section is the finest one with at least one training observation in that window: course × instructor, else course, subject, or all courses. The count of past results is the course × instructor count only, so it is shown only at that level.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'slots',
    h: '5. Time-slot effects',
    blocks: [
      R`Slot key: a first meeting starting at 08:30 or earlier (the 08:15 slot) gets key <code>early</code>, one starting at 18:00 or later (the 19:00 slot) <code>late</code>, any start in between <code>mid</code>; plus <code>_fri</code> if the first listed day is Friday. Six keys: <code>early, mid, late, early_fri, mid_fri, late_fri</code>.`,
      R`<b>Contrasts.</b> Within each (term, window, course) cell that has at least one mid section, each non-mid section @[o]@ is compared with the cell's mid sections:`,
      { ul: [
        R`demand: @[\Delta^d_o=\ln\!\big(1+L_o/S_o\big)-\overline{\ln(1+L_i/S_i)}_{i\in\text{mid}}]@ with @[L=S]@ if filled, else @[B]@ (a lower bound for @[N]@);`,
        R`price (only if @[o]@ filled and the cell has filled mid sections): @[\Delta^p_o=\ln\texttt{mn}_o-\overline{\ln\texttt{mn}_i}_{i\in\text{mid, filled}}]@.`,
      ] },
      R`<b>Estimates.</b> For slot key @[k]@ with @[n_k]@ contrasts:`,
      { math: R`\beta^d_k=\mathrm{round}_{0.1}\Big(2\cdot\overline{\Delta^d}\cdot\tfrac{n_k}{n_k+30}\Big),\qquad \beta^p_k=\overline{\Delta^p}\cdot\tfrac{n_k}{n_k+30}.` },
      R`The factor 2 is because the proxy is compressed relative to @[\log\lambda]@. Estimates are made once over all training rows (pooled) and again per window. A window uses its own value when it has at least 30 contrasts for that key, else the pooled one. Contrasts are not time-weighted.`,
      R`<b>Use.</b> In fitting, each observation is moved to the mid scale: its demand likelihood is evaluated at @[\lambda e^{\beta^d}]@ and its price @[y]@ has @[\beta^p]@ subtracted. At prediction the section's own slot effect is added back (section 3). Calibration keeps slots on unless the same-course ordering accuracy drops by more than 2 points or the Brier score worsens by more than 0.0002; they were kept.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'simulation',
    h: '6. Simulation',
    blocks: [
      R`The site draws the replays in your browser from the seven fitted numbers. Inputs: seats @[S]@ (live seats left for the section), group size @[g]@ (1 to 6), floor @[F]@, the seven parameters, the number of runs (20,000) and a fixed seed.`,
      R`For each replay, with @[k=S-g+1]@:`,
      { ul: [
        R`Draw @[N\sim\mathrm{NegBin}(\lambda,r)]@ as @[\mathrm{Poisson}\big(G\cdot\lambda/r\big)]@, @[G\sim\mathrm{Gamma}(r,1)]@.`,
        R`If @[N<k]@ (or @[k\le0]@ and @[N=0]@), the seats are not contested: cutoff @[=F]@. The replay counts as filled only if @[N\ge k]@.`,
        R`Otherwise draw the level shock @[M=m\,e^{s_M Z}]@ and @[N]@ bids (anchored or lognormal, section 3), round, floor at @[F]@.`,
        R`Cutoff @[=]@ the @[k]@-th highest of the @[N]@ bids. If @[k\le0]@, cutoff @[=F]@.`,
      ] },
      R`So a section with @[S]@ seats clears at the @[S]@-th highest bid among the other bidders, and at the minimum bid when fewer than @[S]@ bid. A group of @[g]@ friends who all bid @[b]@ all get in exactly when fewer than @[k=S-g+1]@ other bidders bid above @[b]@.`,
      R`<b>Outputs.</b> Sort the cutoffs @[C_1\le\dots\le C_R]@. The chance the section fills is @[\hat P_{\text{fill}}=\#\{\text{filled}\}/R]@. The median and 90th percentile come from the sorted cutoffs, and the low end of the range is read off the chance curve. The chance of a seat for bid @[b]@ counts ties as half:`,
      { math: R`\mathrm{pIn}(b)=\frac{\#\{C_i<b\}+\tfrac12\#\{C_i=b\}}{R},` },
      R`evaluated on up to 120 grid points from @[F]@ to @[\max(F+100,\lceil1.15\,p_{99}\rceil)]@ and linearly interpolated. The recommended bid for a target (80, 90, 95%) is the smallest odd-cent bid with @[\mathrm{pIn}\ge\text{target}]@; odd cents avoid ties with round-number bids. The swap view uses 4,000 runs.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'validation',
    h: '7. Fit, validation and the gate',
    blocks: [
      R`<b>Terms.</b> Calibration on 2024-25 Term 2; test on 2025-26 Term 2; held out 2026-27 Term 1. Each backtest fits only on terms before the target term, forecasts every section in every window of that term (group of 1, seats offered, 1,500 runs) and scores against @[a]@. The figures are in the accuracy section above.`,
      R`<b>Calibration</b> uses the validation term only: @[r\in\{0.4,1,2.5\}]@ by Brier score of @[\Pr(\text{fill})]@; @[c_w\in\{0,1\}]@ and three alternative @[\tau_d]@ sets, each kept only if the Brier score improves by more than @[10^{-4}]@; the price scale multiplied twice by @[\exp(\mathrm{median}\ln(a/\text{cond. }p_{50}))]@ over filled sections; @[\ell_{sd}\in\{0.1,0.25,0.4\}]@ with filled-section 80% coverage closest to 0.8; slot effects on or off. Result: @[r=0.4]@, @[c_w=0.5]@, @[\tau]@ as in section 4, price scale 1.350, @[\ell_{sd}=0.1]@, slots on.`,
      R`<b>Metrics.</b>`,
      { ul: [
        R`Average miss over all forecasts: @[\overline{|p_{50}-a|}]@. On sections that filled it uses the median of the replays that filled (conditional @[p_{50}]@), scored on the filled sections the baseline could also forecast. This is the "average miss" on the page.`,
        R`Coverage of the 80% range over all forecasts: the share where the randomized probability integral transform lies in @[[0.1,0.9]]@, with @[u=\Pr(C<a)+U\cdot\Pr(C=a)]@ (ties at the floor would make the literal @[p_{10}\le a\le p_{90}]@ test read far too high). On filled sections: the share with @[p_{10}\le a\le p_{90}]@, which is the page's "real bid inside 80% range".`,
        R`Baseline, "reuse last term's clearing bid": for the same window, the clearing bid of the same section, else the same course and instructor, else the same course, taken from the latest earlier term of the same type (Term 1 for a Term 1 forecast), else the latest earlier term.`,
        R`Brier score of @[\Pr(\text{fill})]@ against the fill outcome, versus a hard baseline and a climatology (weighted fill rate per window).`,
      ] },
      R`<b>The gate.</b> On the test term, the forecasts go live only if both hold: coverage in range (@[0.75\le\text{coverage}_{80}\le0.85]@ over all forecasts and @[0.70\le\text{coverage}_{80}\le0.90]@ on filled sections), and they beat the baseline (model average miss below the baseline's on the same samples, filled-section average miss below the baseline's, and Brier score below both the hard and climatology baselines). Otherwise the parameters are rejected and no forecasts are shown. The held-out term is reported but is not part of the gate. Per window, confidence is "ok" only if filled-section coverage is within 70 to 90% on at least 50 filled sections, else "low".`,
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'swap-formula',
    h: '8. Swap chance',
    blocks: [
      R`Site-only, nothing fitted. A holder of section A asks about section B of the same course.`,
      R`<b>Desirability @[d]@</b>, on the Round 1 Window 1 forecast:`,
      { ul: [
        R`Normal course: @[v_s=\ln p_{50,s}+0.25\ln(\lambda_s/S_s)]@. @[p_{50,s}]@ is the simulated median clearing bid for a group of 1 at the section's full seat count (4,000 runs). The second term breaks ties between sections that all clear at the minimum.`,
        R`Pre-assigned course: @[v_s=\ln(\text{price level})]@ from the same window's forecast, which already includes the slot effect; no simulation.`,
        R`@[d_s=v_s-\mathrm{median}_s(v)]@. A section with no usable forecast uses last term's clearing bids: the log of its matched median winning bid over the course median, or 0 if nothing matches.`,
      ] },
      R`<b>Pre-assigned detection.</b> Over Round 1 Window 1 history rows with vacancy above 0 and seats known before the window, a course is pre-assigned if the median of @[\texttt{before}/\texttt{vacancy}]@ is under 0.25, with at least 3 rows.`,
      R`<b>Holders.</b> @[n=\lfloor\text{seats}\rfloor]@; in a pre-assigned course @[n=\max\big(1,\ n-\mathrm{round}(\mathrm{median}(\texttt{before}))\big)]@, using that section's own Round 1 Window 1 rows, else the course's.`,
      R`<b>Request probability.</b> Each holder of A applies for B with probability`,
      { math: R`p(A\to B)=r\,\sigma_{\text{logistic}}\big(k\,(d_B-d_A)\big),\qquad r=0.1,\ k=5.5.` },
      R`<b>Direct-pair chance.</b> @[X\sim\mathrm{Bin}(n_A-1,\,p_{AB})]@ other holders of A apply for B, and @[Y\sim\mathrm{Bin}(n_B,\,p_{BA})]@ holders of B apply for A. Up to @[\min(1+X,Y)]@ swaps can happen and the applicants from A share them equally:`,
      { math: R`P(\text{swap})=E\!\left[\frac{\min(1+X,\ Y)}{1+X}\right]=\sum_{x}\Pr(X=x)\,\frac{1}{x+1}\sum_{j=1}^{\min(x+1,\,n_B)}\Pr(Y\ge j),` },
      R`using @[E[\min(m,Y)]=\sum_{j=1}^m\Pr(Y\ge j)]@, exact in @[O(n_A+n_B)]@. Cycles through three or more sections are ignored.`,
      R`<b>Band and label.</b> Evaluate at the centre @[(r,k)=(0.1,5.5)]@ and the four corners @[r\in\{r/1.5,\,1.5r\}]@, @[k\in\{k/1.25,\,1.25k\}]@; low and high are the min and max. The label comes from the central chance only: likely if at least 0.75, unlikely if under 0.4, else possible; unknown if either side has no seats.`,
      R`<b>The settings are judgment, not fitted.</b> SMU publishes no swap outcomes. @[r]@ and @[k]@ were set so the central chance lands on target: about 60 to 70% when equally wanted, 80 to 90% when the other section is less wanted, 20 to 40% when more wanted, under 15% when much more wanted. Checked for @[n_A=n_B=45]@, with @[\Delta d=d_B-d_A]@:`,
      { table: { head: [R`@[\Delta d]@`, '−0.25', '−0.10', '0', '+0.20', '+0.40'], rows: [
        ['central chance', '92.6%', '79.8%', '64.4%', '29.8%', '10.7%'],
        ['band', '81 to 99%', '68 to 90%', '57 to 71%', '22 to 39%', '6 to 17%'],
      ] } },
    ],
  },
];

const limits: string[] = [
  'Past results only. No information on this term\'s drops, released seats or coordinator changes; later windows offer only newly freed seats.',
  R`Hierarchy reuse: every observation sits in all four levels, and a child's prior mean is its parent's posterior, which already contains the child's data. The child's data is counted at both levels, so this is not a strict Bayesian hierarchy.`,
  R`The chance of filling is over-confident in the middle: sections forecast with a middling chance of filling filled less often than predicted. The 80% range held for filled sections in both tests; windows from Round 2 Window 3 onward have few filled sections and are marked low confidence.`,
  R`The price model is one lognormal plus a spike at the minimum; bidders who copy "last median plus e$1 to 2" and round-number bids are not modelled beyond the anchor share.`,
  R`Demand and price are fitted per window with no link between windows, and @[\sigma]@, @[q]@, @[s]@ are shared by all courses in a window. @[s]@ is also shared across windows, and its residuals ignore the slot effect. A section whose instructor has never taught the course falls back to the course.`,
  R`Slot effects use crude proxies for demand (a lower bound of @[N]@, times 2) and are shrunk by @[n/(n+30)]@ with no uncertainty carried forward. They are fitted per window, with the pooled value as fallback.`,
  'Seats for some sections are estimated from the previous window and can be wrong.',
  R`At a bid exactly equal to the floor, the chance counts uncontested replays as a tie and so reads half of their weight; one cent above the floor it reads in full. The recommended odd-cent bids avoid this.`,
  'The pre-assigned course path and every swap number rest on judgment settings, with no outcome data to test them.',
];

// Worked example, read from the course file and simulated here with the same code the course page runs.
const CODE = 'MGMT102';
const SECTION = 'G9';
const course = getCourse(CODE);
const sec = course?.sectionsNow.find((s) => s.section === SECTION);
type Col = { window: string; seats: number; note: string; f: ForecastWindow; res: ReturnType<typeof simulate> };
const cols: Col[] = [];
if (sec?.forecast) {
  const picks: [string, number, string][] = [['Round 1 Window 1', sec.seatsTotal, `${sec.seatsTotal} seats`]];
  const lw = sec.live?.window;
  if (lw && lw !== 'Round 1 Window 1' && sec.live) picks.push([lw, sec.live.available, `${sec.live.available} ${sec.live.available === 1 ? 'seat' : 'seats'} left (live)`]);
  for (const [w, seats, note] of picks) {
    const f = sec.forecast[w];
    if (!f || seats <= 0) continue;
    cols.push({ window: w, seats, note, f, res: simulate({ seats, group: 1, minBidCents: sec.minBidCents ?? 1000, params: toSimParams(f), runs: FORECAST_RUNS, seed: FORECAST_SEED }) });
  }
}
const slotLabel = sec?.meetings[0] ? `${sec.meetings[0].day} ${sec.meetings[0].start}` : '';
const n2 = (x: number, d = 2) => x.toFixed(d);
---
<div class="formula-body">
  <p>Status as of 9 November 2026. Python fits, per section and window, seven parameters and writes them to a file; the site then replays the window from those seven numbers.</p>
  <p set:html={rich(R`Notation: @[S]@ seats offered, @[N]@ bidders, @[\lambda=E[N]]@, @[r]@ dispersion of the bidder count, @[m]@ median bid level (cents), @[\sigma]@ bid spread, @[q]@ anchor share, @[F]@ the minimum bid (floor) in cents.`)} />
  <p class="src">Source code: <a href="https://github.com/boeyboeyboeyboey/Bidwise" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/boeyboeyboeyboey/Bidwise</a>, in <code>model/forecast/fit.py</code>, <code>model/forecast/sim.py</code>, <code>site/src/lib/sim/index.ts</code> and <code>site/src/lib/swap/swap.ts</code>.</p>

  {sections.map((s) => (
    <section id={s.id} class="fs">
      <h3>{s.h}</h3>
      {s.blocks.map((b) =>
        typeof b === 'string' ? <p set:html={rich(b)} />
        : 'ul' in b ? <ul>{b.ul.map((li) => <li set:html={rich(li)} />)}</ul>
        : 'math' in b ? <div class="math" set:html={tex(b.math, true)} />
        : (
          <div class="scroll"><table>
            <thead><tr>{b.table.head.map((c, i) => <th class={i > 0 && b.table.head.length > 2 ? 'r' : ''} set:html={rich(c)} />)}</tr></thead>
            <tbody>{b.table.rows.map((row) => <tr>{row.map((c, i) => <td class={i > 0 && row.length > 2 ? 'r' : ''} set:html={rich(c)} />)}</tr>)}</tbody>
          </table></div>
        ))}

    </section>
  ))}

  {cols.length > 0 && (
    <section id="example" class="fs">
      <h3>9. Worked example: {CODE} section {SECTION}{slotLabel && `, ${slotLabel}`}</h3>
      <p set:html={rich(R`Fitted parameters read from this site's course data, then simulated with ${FORECAST_RUNS.toLocaleString('en-US')} runs, a group of 1, and the same code the course page runs. The floor is @[F=${sec!.minBidCents ?? 1000}]@ cents.`)} />
      <div class="scroll"><table>
        <thead><tr><th></th>{cols.map((c) => <th class="r">{c.window}</th>)}</tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td set:html={rich(R`@[\lambda]@`)} />{cols.map((c) => <td class="r">{n2(c.f.demandMean)}</td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td set:html={rich(R`@[m]@ (cents)`)} />{cols.map((c) => <td class="r">{n2(c.f.priceLevelCents, 1)}</td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td set:html={rich(R`@[\sigma]@`)} />{cols.map((c) => <td class="r">{c.f.priceSigma}</td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td set:html={rich(R`@[q]@`)} />{cols.map((c) => <td class="r">{c.f.anchorShare}</td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td set:html={rich(R`@[r]@`)} />{cols.map((c) => <td class="r">{c.f.demandDispersion}</td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td set:html={rich(R`@[s_M]@`)} />{cols.map((c) => <td class="r">{c.f.anchorSigma}</td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td>Past results behind it</td>{cols.map((c) => <td class="r">{c.f.pooledLevel === 'course×instructor' ? `${c.f.nObs}, this course and instructor` : 'none for this instructor'}</td>)}</tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <div class="scroll"><table>
        <thead><tr><th></th>{cols.map((c) => <th class="r">{c.window}, {c.note}</th>)}</tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td set:html={rich(R`@[\Pr(\text{fill})]@`)} />{cols.map((c) => <td class="r">{pct(c.res.pFill)}</td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td set:html={rich(R`@[p_{50}]@`)} />{cols.map((c) => <td class="r">{eS(c.res.percentilesCents.p50)}</td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td set:html={rich(R`@[p_{90}]@`)} />{cols.map((c) => <td class="r">{eS(c.res.percentilesCents.p90)}</td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td>Chance at e$15.00</td>{cols.map((c) => <td class="r">{pct(pInAt(c.res, 1500))}</td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td>Chance at e$30.00</td>{cols.map((c) => <td class="r">{pct(pInAt(c.res, 3000))}</td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td>Bid for 90%</td>{cols.map((c) => <td class="r">{eS(recommendBid(c.res, 0.9))}</td>)}</tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>{cols[0].window}: only {pct(cols[0].res.pFill)} of replays fill, so the median cutoff is just {eS(cols[0].res.percentilesCents.p50)}. A bid of e$15.00 beats the cutoff in {pct(pInAt(cols[0].res, 1500))} of replays (ties count as half), and e$30.00 in {pct(pInAt(cols[0].res, 3000))}.{cols[1] && ` ${cols[1].window}: ${cols[1].seats === 1 ? 'the one remaining seat is' : `the ${cols[1].seats} remaining seats are`} contested by about ${Math.round(cols[1].f.demandMean)} bidders with a wide bid spread (σ = ${cols[1].f.priceSigma}), so the chance climbs slowly with the bid.`}</p>
    </section>
  )}

  <section id="not-used" class="fs">
    <h3>10. Known limits</h3>
    <ul>{limits.map((l) => <li set:html={rich(l)} />)}</ul>
  </section>
</div>

